# Gotchas

Real mistakes conjure's own Flask templates hit while being built and
live-verified — not hypothetical, each one broke something concrete
before it was fixed. Worth knowing before you hit the same wall.

{% if template == "rest-api" %}
## Why `main.py` is a factory, not a module-level `app`

The naive version of this file is:

```python
app = Flask(__name__)
app.config["SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI"] = settings.database_url
db.init_app(app)
```

That looks harmless, but it runs the instant this module is
**imported** — and `pytest`, `flask db migrate`/`upgrade`, and `alembic
revision --autogenerate` all import `{{ package_name }}.main` (directly
or transitively) before doing anything else. With a module-level `app`,
every one of those commands would try to reach whatever
`DATABASE_URL` is configured in `.env` — a real Postgres you may not
have running locally, or one you very much don't want a test run
mutating.

The fix is `create_app(*, database_url: str | None = None, testing:
bool = False) -> Flask`: building the app — and opening the database
connection — only happens when something actually *calls* the factory,
never at import time. Concretely:

- `flask run`/`flask db ...` auto-detect `create_app` from `--app
  src/{{ package_name }}/main.py` — Flask calls it for you.
{% if docker %}
- the Dockerfile's `gunicorn` CMD points at
  `{{ package_name }}.main:create_app()` — same reason.
{% endif %}
- `tests/conftest.py` calls `create_app(database_url="sqlite:///:memory:",
  testing=True)` directly, so the suite never touches `.env`'s
  database at all (see `guides/testing.md`).

**Never add a module-level `app = create_app()` back into `main.py`** to
make an import or a one-off script more convenient — that reintroduces
exactly the bug above. If something outside `flask run`/gunicorn/tests
needs an app instance, it should call `create_app()` itself, in its own
code, not rely on one sitting at module scope.

{% if database == "none" %}
This template has `database=none` — there's no database connection to
protect against yet, but the factory shape is still there so adding one
later (`conjure`'s `database`/`orm` options) doesn't require restructuring
`main.py`.
{% endif %}
{% else %}
## No application factory here — and that's fine, for now

`main.py` is just `app = Flask(__name__)` at module scope, unlike the
`rest-api` template's `create_app()` factory. That's not an
inconsistency: the factory pattern exists specifically to stop a
database connection (or broker, or any other I/O) from being opened the
instant the module is *imported* — by `pytest`, a migration tool,
anything. This template never touches a database or does I/O at import
time, so there's nothing for a factory to protect against.

If you add something here that *does* open a connection or hit the
network at module scope, that's the signal to switch to the same
`create_app()` shape the `rest-api` template uses — see the `flask`
`rest-api` template's `main.py` (or generate a throwaway `rest-api`
project) for the pattern.
{% endif %}

{% if template == "rest-api" and database != "none" %}
## `:memory:` SQLite needs `StaticPool`, or your data vanishes mid-test

A SQLite `:memory:` database lives only as long as the single
connection that created it — the default connection pool hands out a
*new* connection per checkout, so a session opened by one request
can't see rows written by another. `core/db.py` special-cases this: any
time the configured URL contains `:memory:`, it sets
`poolclass=StaticPool` so the whole app (and test run) shares one
connection instead. A real, file-based SQLite database or Postgres
never needs this — only `:memory:`, which in this project only ever
happens in `tests/conftest.py`. If you ever build your own
engine/connection outside `core/db.py`, carry this over or you'll see
tests that pass in isolation but fail when run together (or vice
versa).

{% if migrations %}
## The database is *not* auto-created when migrations are on

This project has `migrations=true`: `create_app()` initializes the
{% if orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}`db`/`migrate` extensions{% else %}database engine{% endif %} but does **not** call
`{% if orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}db.create_all(){% else %}Base.metadata.create_all(engine){% endif %}`
outside of `testing=True` — schema comes solely from
{% if orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}`flask db upgrade`{% else %}`alembic upgrade head`{% endif %}. If you're seeing
"table does not exist" errors against a real (non-test) database,
that's why: run the migration first (see `README.md`'s "Migrations"
section), don't add a `create_all()` call back into `create_app()`'s
non-testing path to "fix" it — the moment `create_all()` and a real
migration history coexist, the tables already exist by the time
{% if orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}`flask db migrate`{% else %}`alembic revision --autogenerate`{% endif %} runs, so it permanently
reports no schema changes instead of capturing the real one — a
project that looks like it has migrations but doesn't.
{% else %}
## Turning migrations on later means removing the `create_all()` call

This project has `migrations=false`: `create_app()` calls
`{% if orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}db.create_all(){% else %}Base.metadata.create_all(engine){% endif %}` on every
boot, so the schema always matches the current models — convenient,
but only correct because there's no migration history to fall out of
sync with. If you regenerate with `migrations=true` later, that call
must go away entirely; `create_all()` and a real migration history must
never coexist (see the migrations-enabled branch of this file for why).
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

## WSGI is sync — a blocking call blocks the whole worker

Flask here runs on WSGI (via the `flask run` dev server, or gunicorn's
sync worker in production), not ASGI. Route handlers are plain `def`,
never `async def`, and there's no `await` anywhere in this codebase's
routes — writing `async def` on a handler doesn't make Flask run it
concurrently the way FastAPI would; it just changes what the return
value has to be, which nothing here expects. A slow call inside a
handler (a slow query, an unthrottled outbound HTTP call) blocks that
worker for the request's whole duration; concurrency here comes from
running more worker **processes/threads** (gunicorn's `--workers`/
`--threads`), not from cooperative async I/O. If a route needs to do
something slow without blocking a request, that's what the {% if worker == "celery" %}Celery
worker (`tasks/`){% else %}background-worker option (`worker=celery` at generation time){% endif %} is for, not an `async def` handler.

## `flask run`'s dev server vs. gunicorn

`flask run` (what `README.md`/`AGENTS.md` tell you to use for local
development) is single-process, reloads on file changes, and is
explicitly documented by Flask as unfit for production — it's not
hardened or tuned for concurrent load.{% if docker %} The Dockerfile never uses it: its
`CMD` runs `gunicorn --bind 0.0.0.0:8000
{{ package_name }}.main:create_app()` instead, gunicorn's sync worker
model, pointed at the same factory `flask run` auto-detects. If you add
a flag/behavior that only makes sense for local dev (debug toasts,
verbose reload logging), put it behind `settings.debug`/`app.debug`,
not directly in `create_app()` unconditionally — the same factory
boots both the dev server and gunicorn.{% else %}If you add `--docker` to this project, that's what its Dockerfile's `CMD`
will run instead of `flask run` — gunicorn, pointed at the same
`create_app` factory, not the dev server.{% endif %}
